; Example usage of the `xref` plugin. Each variant of `:cross-ref` is
; exercised:
;
;   * bare cross-ref — `(edge :from a)` references a declared `(node :id a)`
;   * acyclic — `(node :parent a)` chains nodes without cycling
;   * scope-form — `(edge :phrase intro)` references a `(phrase :name intro)`
;     declared inside the enclosing `(piece …)`
;   * provider — `(edge :uniform u_time)` references a name the `uniforms`
;     provider extracts from `(shader :code …)`. This fixture declares that
;     provider without an `:impl` (export never runs one), so a host
;     validating this document reports `cross_ref_provider_unavailable` at
;     the shader and stays silent at the reference — loud once at the
;     source, not once per use.
;   * target group — `(edge :group-pipeline blit)` references either kind of
;     pipeline through ONE namespace. Read the two sections at the bottom
;     together: the union and the group answer the same question, and the
;     answers differ exactly where a name is defined twice.

(node :id a :label "Origin")
(node :id b :parent a :label "Branch")

(shader :name main :code "uniform float u_time;\nuniform vec2 u_res;")

(piece :title "demo"
  (phrase :name intro :body "rest 4")
  (phrase :name coda :body "tutti")
  (edge :from a :to b :phrase intro :uniform u_time))

; --- Two targets, one name ------------------------------------------------
;
; `:pipeline` is a union over two cross-ref kinds, render first. These two
; declarations are BOTH legal: duplicate names are caught per target, and
; these are two different targets, so each `blit` / `reduce` is alone in its
; own namespace.

(render-pipeline  :name blit)
(compute-pipeline :name reduce)

(edge :from a :to b :pipeline blit)     ; the render pipeline
(edge :from a :to b :pipeline reduce)   ; the compute pipeline

; Now give them the same name. Still no duplicate — still two namespaces —
; but the reference below now resolves in both, and the only thing choosing
; between them is that `render-pipeline-ref` is listed first in the union.

(render-pipeline  :name shared)
(compute-pipeline :name shared)

(edge :from a :to b :pipeline shared)   ; union_ambiguous (warning)

; The document above VALIDATES: `union_ambiguous` is a warning, first match
; still wins, and `shared` here is the render pipeline. What the warning
; says is that a second reading exists — so a tool resolving `shared`
; through its own table instead of by alternative order would pick the
; compute pipeline, and neither it nor the validator would notice they
; disagreed.
;
; Two repairs, and which is right depends on what you meant: rename one
; declaration (the collision was an accident), or split `:pipeline` into two
; single-kind keys (both names are deliberate and the slot was overloaded).

; --- The same two targets, one namespace ----------------------------------
;
; `:group-pipeline` is `(cross-ref :target [render-pipeline compute-pipeline])`
; — not a union of two cross-refs, but one cross-ref over two forms. Both
; names below resolve through it, and neither reference needs to know which
; form defined the name it points at.

(edge :from a :to b :group-pipeline blit)     ; the render pipeline
(edge :from a :to b :group-pipeline reduce)   ; the compute pipeline

; What the group changes is `shared`, declared twice above. Through
; `:pipeline` it is two names in two namespaces and every reference is
; `union_ambiguous`; through `:group-pipeline` it is one name defined twice
; in one namespace, which is `duplicate_cross_ref_target` — reported once,
; at the declarations, where the mistake actually is.
;
; So this document carries both diagnostics, and that is the point of
; keeping the two shapes side by side: the warning is about a *reference*
; that has two readings, the error is about *declarations* that collided.
; A schema author picks the group when the names are meant to be unique
; across the forms, and the union when they are not.

(edge :from a :to b :group-pipeline shared)   ; resolves — the collision is
                                              ; reported at the declarations

